/* book Worked example: Q: A Hall plate carries I = 10 mA in a flux density B = 0.1 T. The plate thickness is t = 0.5 mm and carrier density is n = 1×10²² m⁻³. Take q = 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C. Find VH in volts and in millivolts.
   1. Convert: I = 10 mA = 0.010 A; t = 0.5 mm = 0.0005 m.
   2. KH = 1/(n q t) = 1/(1×10²² × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹ × 0.0005) = 1/0.8 = 1.25 m²/C.
   3. VH = KH I B = 1.25 × 0.010 × 0.1 = 0.00125 V.
   4. In millivolts: 0.00125 × 1000 = 1.25 mV.
   Answer: The Hall voltage is 0.00125 V, or 1.25 mV; an amplifier raises this to a logic level. */
