/* book Worked example 2: Q: An ABS wheel-speed sensor reads a tone ring of 48 teeth. The tyre rolling radius is 0.30 m and the car runs at 72 km/h. Find the wheel speed in rpm, the sensor pulse frequency and the time between pulses.
   1. Vehicle speed: v = 72 ÷ 3.6 = 20.0 m/s.
   2. Wheel angular speed: ω = v ÷ r = 20.0 ÷ 0.30 = 66.67 rad/s.
   3. Revolutions per second: n = ω ÷ 2π = 66.67 ÷ 6.283 = 10.61 rev/s, that is 10.61 × 60 = 636.6 rpm.
   4. Pulse frequency: f = z × n = 48 × 10.61 = 509.3 Hz (the same as z n ÷ 60 with n = 636.6 rpm).
   5. Time between pulses: 1 ÷ 509.3 = 1.96 ms.
   6. Check at walking pace, 3.6 km/h: everything scales by 1/20, so f = 25.5 Hz. A VR sensor's voltage also falls by about 20 times, which is why ABS moved to active (Hall or magnetoresistive) wheel sensors that work down to standstill.
   Answer: The wheel turns at 636.6 rpm, the sensor gives 509.3 Hz, and the pulses are 1.96 ms apart. */
