/* book exam traps: A VR sensor is not a Hall sensor: its output is an AC voltage whose size depends on speed, so it needs a threshold and often a zero-crossing detector. A Hall sensor needs a supply; a Hall switch gives a digital signal and a linear Hall sensor an analog one. | Hall voltage is proportional to B, but a Hall switch has hysteresis: the operate and release fields differ. | Convert mA to A and mm to m before using VH = I B/(n q t); a factor of 1000 is the common slip. | Optical sensors fail with dirt, oil and sunlight; a missing pulse may be a blocked beam, not a bad ECU. | A passive VR sensor cannot report zero speed; no rotation means no flux change and no output. */
