/* book Worked example 2: Q: An NTC coolant sensor has R = 2.2 kΩ at 25 °C and B = 3500 K. It sits from the ECU input to ground with a 2.2 kΩ pull-up to 5 V, read by a 10-bit ADC. Using R = R25 exp[B (1/T − 1/298.15)], find the resistance, the divider voltage and the ADC count at 90 °C and at −10 °C.
   1. At 90 °C, T = 363.15 K: 1/T − 1/298.15 = 0.0027537 − 0.0033540 = −0.0006003 K⁻¹.
   2. R = 2200 × exp(3500 × −0.0006003) = 2200 × exp(−2.101) = 2200 × 0.1223 = 269 Ω.
   3. V = 5 × 269 ÷ (269 + 2200) = 0.545 V; count = round(0.545 × 1023 ÷ 5) = 111.
   4. At −10 °C, T = 263.15 K: 1/T − 1/298.15 = 0.0038001 − 0.0033540 = 0.0004461 K⁻¹.
   5. R = 2200 × exp(3500 × 0.0004461) = 2200 × exp(1.561) = 2200 × 4.765 = 10.48 kΩ.
   6. V = 5 × 10 484 ÷ (10 484 + 2200) = 4.133 V; count = round(4.133 × 1023 ÷ 5) = 846.
   7. Check: the hot reading uses only 111 counts, so one count near 90 °C is worth about 0.4 °C. That is why many ECUs switch in a second, smaller pull-up once the engine is warm.
   Answer: 90 °C: 269 Ω, 0.545 V, count 111. −10 °C: 10.48 kΩ, 4.133 V, count 846. The voltage falls as the coolant warms. */
